1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,234
Snakes for hair.

2
00:00:03,534 --> 00:00:06,043
A gaze that turns people to stone.

3
00:00:06,343 --> 00:00:10,607
Medusa is one of the most
famous monsters of Greek myth.

4
00:00:10,907 --> 00:00:13,422
Today, she is also seen as a victim,

5
00:00:13,422 --> 00:00:16,985
a woman punished for something
that was done to her.

6
00:00:17,285 --> 00:00:18,770
So which is she?

7
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:21,921
The answer depends
on which source you read.

8
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:28,950
In this episode, we follow Medusa
across seven centuries of Greek

9
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:34,514
and Roman poetry and art: an early
poem about the gods, faces on ancient

10
00:00:34,514 --> 00:00:39,923
shields and temples, a painted vase
at The Metropolitan Museum of Art,

11
00:00:39,923 --> 00:00:43,685
and a Roman poet who told
her story in a new way.

12
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:55,433
One of the earliest accounts comes from
the Greek poet Hesiod, around 700 BCE.

13
00:00:55,733 --> 00:01:00,557
In his Theogony, a poem about
the origins of the gods, Medusa

14
00:01:00,557 --> 00:01:05,702
is one of three Gorgon sisters,
daughters of ancient sea deities.

15
00:01:06,002 --> 00:01:09,597
Her sisters, Stheno
and Euryale, are immortal.

16
00:01:09,897 --> 00:01:12,063
Medusa alone is mortal.

17
00:01:12,967 --> 00:01:15,614
Hesiod tells us very little about her.

18
00:01:15,914 --> 00:01:21,224
He says that Poseidon lay with her in
a soft meadow among spring flowers,

19
00:01:21,224 --> 00:01:25,871
and that when Perseus cut off
her head, the winged horse Pegasus

20
00:01:25,871 --> 00:01:30,075
and Chrysaor, named for his golden
sword, sprang from her.

21
00:01:30,375 --> 00:01:33,011
There is no curse and no punishment.

22
00:01:33,311 --> 00:01:36,905
In Hesiod, Medusa is simply born a Gorgon.

23
00:01:38,733 --> 00:01:42,935
Early Greek artists gave
the Gorgon an unforgettable face.

24
00:01:43,235 --> 00:01:47,525
In the sixth century BCE, she
often looks like this: wide,

25
00:01:47,525 --> 00:01:52,643
staring eyes, a grinning mouth,
sharp tusks, and a protruding tongue.

26
00:01:52,943 --> 00:01:55,238
She is not meant to be beautiful.

27
00:01:55,538 --> 00:01:57,363
She is meant to be seen.

28
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:02,951
Faces like this, called
gorgoneia, appeared on shields,

29
00:02:02,951 --> 00:02:07,090
on drinking cups, and on the tiles
along temple roofs.

30
00:02:07,390 --> 00:02:10,996
Many scholars think they
were meant to frighten away harm.

31
00:02:11,296 --> 00:02:14,814
The monster's face could
also serve as a guardian.

32
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:18,230
Even the goddess Athena carried it.

33
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:21,897
As early as Homer's
Iliad, the Gorgon's head

34
00:02:21,897 --> 00:02:25,813
appears on the aegis,
Athena's protective goatskin.

35
00:02:26,113 --> 00:02:31,157
The face of the defeated monster
became part of the goddess's own power.

36
00:02:33,033 --> 00:02:36,072
Over time, the image began to change.

37
00:02:36,372 --> 00:02:42,483
Around 490 BCE, the poet Pindar
spoke of fair-cheeked Medusa.

38
00:02:42,783 --> 00:02:46,966
He also told how Athena
invented a melody for the aulos,

39
00:02:46,966 --> 00:02:51,985
a reed pipe, to imitate the lament
of the Gorgons for their sister.

40
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:57,198
On this vase
at The Metropolitan Museum of Art,

41
00:02:57,198 --> 00:03:01,496
painted in Athens around
450 BCE, Perseus comes

42
00:03:01,496 --> 00:03:05,887
upon Medusa as she sleeps,
with Athena close by.

43
00:03:06,187 --> 00:03:10,334
Medusa still has wings, but
her face is young and calm.

44
00:03:10,634 --> 00:03:13,970
The museum describes it
as one of the earliest

45
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:17,009
images of Medusa with an attractive face.

46
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:20,774
Why did artists begin
to show her this way?

47
00:03:21,074 --> 00:03:25,019
Scholars have suggested
several reasons, from changing

48
00:03:25,019 --> 00:03:28,667
ideals of beauty to new
ways of telling the story.

49
00:03:28,967 --> 00:03:31,816
The sources do not give a single answer.

50
00:03:32,116 --> 00:03:37,810
But by the fifth century BCE, Medusa
could be both terrifying and beautiful.

51
00:03:39,633 --> 00:03:43,229
The version best known today
comes later, from Rome.

52
00:03:43,529 --> 00:03:47,391
Around the year 8 CE,
the poet Ovid completed

53
00:03:47,391 --> 00:03:51,955
his Metamorphoses, a long
poem about transformations.

54
00:03:52,255 --> 00:03:55,520
In Book Four, Perseus
himself tells the story.

55
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:59,222
Medusa, he says, was once
a beautiful young

56
00:03:59,222 --> 00:04:02,299
woman, admired above all for her hair.

57
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:07,410
Then, it is said, Neptune,
the Roman name for Poseidon,

58
00:04:07,410 --> 00:04:11,776
assaulted her in the temple
of Minerva, the Roman Athena.

59
00:04:12,076 --> 00:04:15,012
The goddess hid her face behind her aegis.

60
00:04:15,312 --> 00:04:18,419
And so that the act
would not go unpunished,

61
00:04:18,419 --> 00:04:21,164
she changed Medusa's hair into snakes.

62
00:04:22,067 --> 00:04:25,687
Ovid wrote about seven
hundred years after Hesiod,

63
00:04:25,687 --> 00:04:29,233
and he often reshaped
older myths in his own way.

64
00:04:29,533 --> 00:04:32,543
Other ancient writers
tell different stories.

65
00:04:32,843 --> 00:04:38,351
The mythographer known as Apollodorus
reports that, according to some, Medusa

66
00:04:38,351 --> 00:04:44,223
was beheaded for Athena's sake, because
she wished to rival the goddess in beauty.

67
00:04:46,033 --> 00:04:49,947
Medusa's story lived on long
after the ancient world.

68
00:04:50,247 --> 00:04:54,472
In the early 1800s,
the sculptor Antonio Canova

69
00:04:54,472 --> 00:04:58,605
carved Perseus holding
up her head in triumph.

70
00:04:58,905 --> 00:05:02,498
For Medusa's face, Canova
followed an ancient

71
00:05:02,498 --> 00:05:06,092
marble mask of a beautiful,
sorrowful Medusa.

72
00:05:06,392 --> 00:05:09,187
The hero is the center of this statue.

73
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:15,096
In the twentieth century, many
writers and artists returned to Ovid's

74
00:05:15,096 --> 00:05:20,019
version and read Medusa as a woman
who was wronged, and then blamed.

75
00:05:20,319 --> 00:05:23,413
That reading is a modern interpretation.

76
00:05:23,713 --> 00:05:26,211
But it is built on an ancient text.

77
00:05:28,033 --> 00:05:30,894
So is Medusa a monster or a victim?

78
00:05:31,194 --> 00:05:33,979
In Hesiod, she is born a Gorgon.

79
00:05:34,279 --> 00:05:37,806
On early temples and shields,
her face protects.

80
00:05:38,106 --> 00:05:42,247
On a classical vase, she
sleeps with a young, calm face.

81
00:05:42,547 --> 00:05:46,621
And in Ovid, she is a woman
punished for what was done to her.

82
00:05:49,067 --> 00:05:51,906
There is no single original Medusa.

83
00:05:52,206 --> 00:05:55,313
Each source shows how
a different time imagined her.

84
00:05:55,613 --> 00:05:57,930
Every story has an origin.

85
00:05:58,230 --> 00:05:59,993
Medusa has many.

86
00:06:03,567 --> 00:06:08,396
This video is based on ancient
texts and public museum collections.

87
00:06:08,696 --> 00:06:11,653
The narration uses synthetic speech,

88
00:06:11,653 --> 00:06:15,595
and some images are
AI-generated reconstructions.

89
00:06:15,895 --> 00:06:19,851
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

90
00:06:20,151 --> 00:06:21,923
Thank you for watching.
